# Every text file in this repository is stored and checked out as LF, on
# every platform. Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true system-wide, and
# without this line a Windows clone gets CRLF working-tree copies of files
# whose blobs are LF. Git then fails its own CRLF round-trip check on them,
# so `git status` reports AGENTS.md, README.md, CHANGELOG.md, ROADMAP.md,
# SECURITY.md, THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md and skills/README.md as modified
# forever while `git diff` shows nothing and the blob hashes are identical.
# The first real edit to any of them then arrives as a whole-file diff.
#
# This is the same defect EIF fixes for the project instances it generates
# (the managed .gitattributes block, 0.2.2). The framework's own repository
# had it too. Adding this changes no blob: every tracked text file here is
# already LF in the index, and the five tracked PNGs are detected as binary
# by text=auto and are left alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Kept explicit even though the line above already covers it. Benchmark
# fixtures record an immutable sha256 source_digest over their exact bytes
# (core/schemas/benchmark-fixture-manifest.schema.json) - that digest is only
# reproducible across machines if every checkout produces identical bytes.
# Without a pin, core.autocrlf=true converts these files to CRLF on checkout
# while the git blob itself stays LF, so a fresh Windows clone's
# source_digest would never match what was recorded (Linux/CI checkouts never
# convert, so this never surfaced there) - a real bug found via
# `eifctl_benchmark.py validate-manifest` failing after a routine `git rebase`
# on Windows. The pin stays so that a future change to the wildcard above
# cannot silently unpin the one place where bytes are a recorded claim.
docs/benchmarks/fixtures/** text eol=lf
